WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.768
How do you get better at small talk?

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:05.038
Because as an introvert,
it can be hard work.

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:08.675
I'm going to talk about the three things
that you probably haven't heard of of how

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:13.680
to make small talk feel easy, effortless,
and kind of like all the naturals do it.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.749
small talk as an introvert

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:19.619
is one of the hardest things to get over
because it just sucks.

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:20.520
I want to go deep.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.856
I want to jump in.
I want to skip the foreplay.

00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:23.790
But you can't.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.191
Not in this society.

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.494
There's a lot of people
who need something more.

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:30.530
And so I went on a little bit of research
to find out what is it?

00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:33.133
How do you actually get better at Small
talk?

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:34.768
And I came up with these three things.

00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:35.935
That's
what I'm going to share in this video.

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:37.237
Stick around for number three.

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:39.873
I promise
that one is the most important one.

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:44.210
And it took me forever to learn it because
I thought only one and two mattered.

00:00:44.644 --> 00:00:46.379
But it's more than that. So here we go.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:51.251
Number one, It's the belief
that anybody has common interest with you.

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:52.152
It's the belief

00:00:52.152 --> 00:00:55.822
that there's always something in common
between you and another person.

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:58.958
It doesn't matter how different they are,
doesn't matter who they are.

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:03.730
There is always something in common in
your job is to be that bloodhound,

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:07.500
to get after the scent
and find that thing that you,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.938
as human beings on this spinning
rock, have in common with one another,

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:15.375
and reframing it as it's always possible
and there's always

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:18.711
something is much, much better than man,
I don't like this dude.

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:20.580
This dude's kind of bumming me out.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.182
Don't have anything in common.

00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:24.784
No, you do, but you have to find it.

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:26.286
And that leads us to tip number two.

00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:27.020
How do you find it?

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.356
Well,
you got to find the why behind the what?

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:34.094
So they like tennis and you like reading.

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:37.163
Maybe they're two different things,
but there is a reason

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:40.066
that they like what they like.
And you like what you like.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:42.569
Can you find the why behind the what?

00:01:42.569 --> 00:01:46.039
Maybe they like reading
because it puts them in a state of flow

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:48.441
and the world
can kind of disappear for an hour.

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.009
Which is exactly why I love tennis,

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:52.979
because I can just get on the court
and just be in the zone.

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:55.482
And for one hour nothing else matters.

00:01:55.482 --> 00:01:56.416
And maybe that

00:01:56.416 --> 00:02:00.286
feeling of I just want to escape
for a little bit and be in my own bubble.

00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:03.356
Hey, maybe that's what we have in common
as introverts, I don't know.

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:05.758
Find the why behind the what.

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:08.828
Video games
and cooking are two different things,

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:12.065
but maybe it's all about feeling
immersed in an activity.

00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:15.068
You always have something in common
with somebody.

00:02:15.068 --> 00:02:18.905
As long as you start to find out
why people do what they do.

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:21.474
Simon Sinek has that great Ted
talk on this.

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:22.575
Start with why.

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:25.145
But it doesn't just apply to business
and marketing.

00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:29.849
It's a basic human fundamental idea
that it's not about what we do.

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:34.454
It's about the motivations that drive
the things that make us human.

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:36.689
But what that

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:40.627
leads to is asking a lot of questions,
being curious.

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:43.196
These are all great things,
and we know this.

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:47.467
However, we can fall into a trap
where all we do is ask questions,

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:51.371
be curious, get to know,
keep, keep probing, keep asking.

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:54.174
And by the end of the night, you've
learned so much about the other person.

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:56.509
They haven't learned anything about you.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:57.644
Does that sound familiar?

00:02:57.644 --> 00:03:00.647
Because that's been my life for ever.

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:04.651
And it's frustrating
because what if somebody else isn't?

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:08.054
A great question
asker isn't curious about you?

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:11.591
I start to feel like, man,
I put in all that legwork.

00:03:12.125 --> 00:03:14.727
Come on, let's let's make it 5050.

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:16.529
But not everybody is like that.

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:18.264
And that's why it's so important.

00:03:18.264 --> 00:03:21.434
Tip number three own your interests.

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:23.736
You got to deliver.

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:25.972
You got to bring yourself
to the conversation

00:03:25.972 --> 00:03:28.608
and talk what you want to talk about.

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:32.212
That's the hardest thing for me
is to actually say, you know what

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:36.249
that reminds me of a book I'm reading
and let me tell you about it

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:40.820
without asking, without without requesting
permission, I'm just going to decide.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.188
We're going to talk a little bit
about what

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:44.357
I want to talk about,
and I'll get some feelers out.

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:48.528
if you want to enjoy speaking, then
you got to speak about what you enjoy.

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:50.597
It's the only way.

00:03:50.597 --> 00:03:53.633
Small talk for it
to be really something you like.

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:56.035
You got to like talking about something

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:59.172
you enjoy, like
it's got to also fulfill you.

00:03:59.172 --> 00:04:02.275
So talk about the video games, talk
about the book you're reading.

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:05.979
Talk about what you saw on TV last night,
bring up stuff

00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:07.780
you enjoy because you never know.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.716
Other people might enjoy it too,

00:04:09.716 --> 00:04:12.018
and it might spark
even more interesting direction.

00:04:12.018 --> 00:04:16.089
So take that confidence
and own your own interest.

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:18.324
Don't just be curious about other people.

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:20.727
That's what most introverts tend to do.

00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:24.030
Also, have the courage to assert yourself
and bring up

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:27.033
some conversation topics of your own
that you want to talk about.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:30.937
Those are the three tips, and hopefully
your small talk will get better

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.305
with a little bit of practice.

00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:36.142
Just remember to own your interests
because it's hard for us

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:39.145
introverts to do it,
but it's going to pay off in the end.

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:40.313
Thanks for watching!

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:42.515
Please subscribe
and I'll see you next time.

